---
/*
 * TRANSPARENCY, the foundation: the policy every see-through surface in the system follows. It is
 * applied through `appearance="frosted"` on each component. There is no standalone class: the old
 * `.sk-translucent` (patterns/transparency.css) was removed once every surface it styled published
 * frosted.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import "@skryensya/core/patterns/box.css";

const react = `import { Navbar, NavbarBrand } from "@skryensya/react/navbar";
import { Box } from "@skryensya/react/box";

<Navbar appearance="frosted">
  <NavbarBrand>Atlas</NavbarBrand>
</Navbar>

<Box as="article" appearance="frosted" surface="surface" padding="lg">
  <h2>Activity</h2>
  <p>12 changes since your last visit.</p>
</Box>;`;

const html = `<article
  class="sk-box"
  data-appearance="frosted"
  data-surface="surface"
  data-padding="lg"
>
  <h2>Weekly summary</h2>
  <p>Content always lives on an opaque baseline.</p>
</article>`;

const customize = `.hero-card {
  /* The tint: the opaque surface the material starts from. */
  --sk-box-bg: var(--color-bg-surface-raised);
}`;

const ownMarkup = `.my-glass {
  /* The material, behind the system switch; the fallback is the opaque baseline. */
  --frost-glass: var(--frost-on) color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-bg-surface) 80%, transparent);
  background: var(--frost-glass, var(--color-bg-surface));
  -webkit-backdrop-filter: var(--frost-on) blur(16px) saturate(150%);
  backdrop-filter: var(--frost-on) blur(16px) saturate(150%);
}`;

---

<Base
  title="Transparency"
  documentLayout="grid"
  description="The policy behind every translucent surface: opaque baseline, progressive blur, reduced transparency, high contrast and a contrast floor."
>
  <h1>Transparency</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A translucent surface lets the context show through without handing it the contrast. In this system,
    transparency is not a class you add: it is a policy every see-through surface follows, and you turn it
    on with <a href="/appearance"><code>appearance="frosted"</code></a>. It always starts from an opaque
    surface. If the browser cannot blur, or the reader asks for less transparency, the content keeps exactly
    the same box and the same reading.
  </p>


  <h2>What's happening</h2>
  <p>
    There are three layers. The <strong>backdrop</strong> is what was already behind; the
    <strong>surface</strong> mixes its opaque colour with alpha and blurs that backdrop; the
    <strong>content</strong> sits on top, at 100% opacity. <code>opacity</code> is never applied to the whole
    container, because that would degrade the text, the icons and the borders as well.
  </p>

  <figure class="transparency-lab">
    <div class="transparency-lab__stage">
      <div class="transparency-lab__backdrop" aria-hidden="true">
        <span>ALPHA</span>
        <span>BLUR</span>
        <span>CONTRAST</span>
      </div>

      <div class="transparency-lab__status">
        <span class="transparency-lab__status-fallback">Opaque fallback</span>
        <span class="transparency-lab__status-allowed">Transparency allowed</span>
        <span class="transparency-lab__status-reduced">Reduced transparency</span>
      </div>

      <div class="transparency-lab__samples">
        <article class="sk-box transparency-lab__panel" data-appearance="frosted" data-surface="sunken">
          <code>sunken</code>
          <strong>Recessed</strong>
          <span>The sunken surface, frosted</span>
        </article>
        <article class="sk-box transparency-lab__panel" data-appearance="frosted" data-surface="surface">
          <code>surface</code>
          <strong>Standard</strong>
          <span>The page surface, frosted</span>
        </article>
        <article class="sk-box transparency-lab__panel" data-appearance="frosted" data-surface="raised">
          <code>raised</code>
          <strong>Lifted</strong>
          <span>The raised surface, frosted</span>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figcaption>
      Three frosted Boxes over a calibration background that combines fine lines, colour shifts and type, so
      both the alpha and the blur are visible. Each tints the material with its own surface. With the reduction,
      they return to those same three opaque surfaces.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>The policy</h2>
  <p>Every frosted surface in the system follows these rules, and your own glass should too.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Opaque first.</strong> The baseline is the component's own opaque surface, declared before any
      query. That is what a browser without <code>backdrop-filter</code> gets, and it has to be a finished,
      legible design, not a broken one.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The material is an enhancement, decided once.</strong> Alpha and blur only turn on under
      <code>@supports (backdrop-filter)</code> <em>and</em>
      <code>prefers-reduced-transparency: no-preference</code>. That decision lives in one place, the
      <code>--frost-on</code> switch, and every frosted surface reads it; no component restates it. The system
      never relies on the <code>reduce</code> branch to be safe.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Opacity is a contrast floor, not taste.</strong> A face over an unknown backdrop has to keep its
      label legible over the worst one, so every frosted surface is measured at 4.5:1 over solid black and solid
      white, in both modes: every Button variant and tone, at rest and pressed, and every surface that carries
      text. Neutral faces need about 65 to 75%; toned ones (accent, danger, a toned callout) about 90 to 94%, so
      they let through only a trace. That is why the material has no hooks.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>High contrast outranks the material.</strong> <code>data-contrast="high"</code> and
      <code>prefers-contrast: more</code> both return to the opaque baseline.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Forced colors keep only the boundary.</strong> The face becomes <code>Canvas</code>, the blur and
      the shadows go, and the edge stays.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Content stays at 100%.</strong> Only the surface paint is mixed with <code>transparent</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The blur never animates.</strong> States change the face's density, which is cheap; the blur radius
      stays put.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <div class="sk-callout" data-tone="info">
    <div class="sk-callout__content">
      <p>
        <strong><code>prefers-reduced-transparency</code> is still experimental.</strong> That is why rule 2 turns
        the material on under <code>no-preference</code> instead of turning it off under <code>reduce</code>:
        without any support, the result is opaque.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Use it</h2>
  <p>
    Set <code>appearance="frosted"</code> on the component that paints the surface. It is the same axis as plain,
    tactile and brutalist, so it does not change variant, tone, size, radius or density.
    <a href="/appearance">Appearance</a> lists every component that publishes it.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={react} label="React" lang="tsx" />
  <CodeBlock code={html} label="HTML" lang="html" />

  <h2>Customize without breaking the fallback</h2>
  <p>
    There is one decision left to you: the <strong>tint</strong>, the component's opaque surface
    (<code>surface</code>, or its <code>--sk-*-bg</code> hook). It is what contrast is validated against, and what
    the reduction shows. The material itself (opacity, blur, saturation) is fixed per component, because its
    opacity is the measured contrast floor for that component's text; there is nothing to tune without breaking
    it.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={customize} label="CSS" />

  <h2>When not to use it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>On a flat canvas: there is no useful context to reveal.</li>
    <li>In tables, long forms or dense UI: the backdrop competes with the reading.</li>
    <li>As the only separation between two regions: keep the border, the space or the elevation.</li>
    <li>Applying <code>opacity</code> to the container: that makes the contents translucent too.</li>
    <li>On the state layer: that alpha communicates interaction, it is not a glass surface.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Your own markup</h2>
  <p>
    For a surface the library does not own, read the same switch. <code>--frost-on</code> is a space toggle: on,
    it is empty; off (no <code>backdrop-filter</code>, reduced transparency, high contrast or forced colors), it is
    invalid, so the glass falls back to the opaque surface and the filter to <code>none</code>. Measure your text
    over black and white before you pick the opacity.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={ownMarkup} label="CSS" />

  <h2>What the system contract says</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Dialog and Vaul.</strong> Their backdrops go from a mix with <code>transparent</code> to an
      opaque background. The modality and the drag both stay.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><a href="/gradients">Media gradient</a>.</strong> The wash becomes the opaque base colour
      inside the caption box; it does not cover the whole image.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><a href="/state-layer">State layer</a>.</strong> It is kept, because it is essential feedback;
      selection and focus carry independent indicators of their own.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Blur during a transition.</strong> That belongs to
      <a href="/motion"><code>prefers-reduced-motion</code></a>, not to this surface policy.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Checklist</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The opaque fallback works before any media query.</li>
    <li>The appearance lives on the component that paints the surface.</li>
    <li>Text and icons remain at 100% opacity.</li>
    <li>The border or the elevation goes on separating the surface from what is behind it.</li>
    <li>The UI works even if the browser ignores the preference entirely.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Platform reference: <a
 href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/At-rules/@media/prefers-reduced-transparency"
 >MDN, <code>prefers-reduced-transparency</code></a>.
  </p>
</Base>

<style>
  .transparency-lab {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-stack-sm);
    margin: var(--space-stack-md) 0 var(--space-section);
  }

  .transparency-lab__stage {
    position: relative;
    min-block-size: 30rem;
    padding: clamp(var(--space-inset-md), 5vw, var(--space-inset-xl));
    overflow: clip;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border-subtle);
    border-radius: var(--radius-surface);
    background:
      repeating-linear-gradient(
        0deg,
        color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-text-primary) 18%, transparent) 0 1px,
        transparent 1px 24px
      ),
      repeating-linear-gradient(
        90deg,
        color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-text-primary) 18%, transparent) 0 1px,
        transparent 1px 24px
      ),
      linear-gradient(
        135deg,
        var(--color-bg-accent-subtle) 0 32%,
        var(--color-action-accent) 32% 42%,
        var(--color-bg-warning-subtle) 42% 72%,
        var(--color-bg-success-subtle) 72% 100%
      );
    isolation: isolate;
  }

  .transparency-lab__backdrop {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: -1;
    display: grid;
    align-content: space-between;
    padding: var(--space-inset-md);
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-text-primary) 72%, transparent);
    font-family: var(--font-family-mono);
    font-size: clamp(2.5rem, 10vw, 7rem);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-heading);
    letter-spacing: -0.08em;
    line-height: 0.72;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .transparency-lab__backdrop span:nth-child(2) {
    justify-self: center;
  }

  .transparency-lab__backdrop span:last-child {
    justify-self: end;
  }

  .transparency-lab__status {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    display: inline-flex;
    justify-self: start;
    margin-block-end: var(--space-stack-lg);
    padding: var(--space-inset-xs) var(--space-inset-sm);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border-default);
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    background: var(--color-bg-surface);
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    font-size: var(--font-size-caption);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-label);
  }

  .transparency-lab__status-allowed,
  .transparency-lab__status-reduced {
    display: none;
  }

  .transparency-lab__samples {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-inline-md);
    align-items: end;
  }

  .transparency-lab__panel {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-stack-xs);
    min-block-size: 13rem;
    padding: var(--space-inset-md);
    align-content: end;
    border-radius: var(--radius-surface);
  }

  .transparency-lab__panel code {
    justify-self: start;
    padding: 0.15em 0.45em;
    border-radius: var(--radius-control);
    background: var(--color-bg-surface-sunken);
    color: var(--color-text-accent);
  }

  .transparency-lab__panel strong {
    font-size: var(--font-size-heading-sm);
  }

  .transparency-lab__panel span {
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    font-size: var(--font-size-caption);
  }

  .transparency-lab figcaption {
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    font-size: var(--font-size-caption);
    line-height: var(--font-line-height-caption);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-transparency: no-preference) {
    .transparency-lab__status-fallback {
      display: none;
    }

    .transparency-lab__status-allowed {
      display: inline;
    }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-transparency: reduce) {
    .transparency-lab__status-fallback {
      display: none;
    }

    .transparency-lab__status-reduced {
      display: inline;
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 48rem) {
    .transparency-lab__samples {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .transparency-lab__panel {
      min-block-size: 8rem;
    }
  }
</style>
